  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure, centered over the region this morning, will\nslowly shift eastward and meander off the East Coast through \nearly this week. In addition, upper level ridging will slowly \nbuild northward through midweek. This will allow for generally \ndry conditions to continue across the area. A warm front will \nlift northward across the area on Tuesday which may bring a few \nshowers to areas north and west of the Capital District; \notherwise, no precipitation is expected through Wednesday.\nRising 850 hPa temperatures (to +1-2 STDEV) will bring a period\nof above normal temperatures beginning today and continue \nthrough most of the upcoming week. There continues to be\nincreasing chances for some rain by the end of the week. While \nthe overall synoptic pattern remains a bit uncertain, an \napproaching upper-level trough or closed low from the west \nshould drag a cold front across the region sometime Thursday \ninto Friday which will be accompanied by a period of rain. In \naddition, some additional moisture from a low pressure system \noff the East Coast may also get advected into the region during\nthis time. Nonetheless, this appears to be the next best chance\nfor a more widespread rainfall and finally bring an end to the \nprolonged dry stretch for many areas.",
